's non-zero so it's about 0.7  and again these are all no for those of  you who fall football like a lot of  these names are known as these fast  speedy deep threat receivers so it makes  sense that we're seeing these names  all right so i'm going to first talk  about surfaces so  i'm going to go back  essentially we're taking the we're we're  fitting a a mixed effects model max  sustain speed is our y variable the  variable of interest which in the first  case will be surfaces is the x is that x  variable and then player id remains a  random effect so if we were to do this  and we were to analyze the coefficients  that we we get here so we're accounting  for player differences  um and we're just seeing how max  sustained speed is affected we get this  chart and  the y-axis here are different types of  surface different types of grass less  artificial turf um  the the top section there the first the  ones in the red box those are all types  of turf i don't expect anyone to know  the difference between them i don't know  the difference between them they're just  different um  turf like different slightly different  material from each other  so i decided to differentiate them um  the uh the  the then then like the x axis here  that's that's change in max sustained  speed from grass and grass is our  reference variable reference layer here  so as you can see shaw sports momentum  pro seems to have a big effect on the  biggest effect in a positive direction  in terms of change in max  speed from grass  and the the lowest is grass slash  baseball diamond and for those of you  who maybe watch football there was a  team the the oakland raiders who  had a stadium  in which  part of it  they shared with a baseball  uh team so  during some of the weeks of the season  there was a there was like an outfield  in the middle of the stadium or in the  middle of the field which you can see up  there  uh so  that is that that's what that is um  and again like  we're we're somewhat involved in in the  field surf like like in terms of field  surfaces and this is something that the  league can be involved in  um and then the other uh thing that we  have the other thing that i'm going to  talk about here is is play clock so  again play clock that's the amount of  time that you have to start the the to  take the snap before you would get  penalized  um  and that that's like a a player's tackle  the player goes out of bounds and then  you're able to then you have 40 seconds  before  the offense is penalized if they don't  snap the ball before then so there have  been suggestions to shorten this play  clock make it  um  less so that way maybe you're going to  see more plays it'll go faster  that that's definitely been something  that's been suggested so in a way a way  we can analyze this and again this is a  a simple example we're really only  looking at one component of player  performance here but a way to analyze  this would be if we look at the time  from the previous play and then we look  we look at how the max sustained speed  will change  um based on that we can see that there  is a positive effect in the sense that  if you have more time between the plays  the players are able to reach faster  speeds which intuitively makes a lot of  sense you're going to be less tired  you're going to be less exhausted you  didn't just  if you have more time to catch your  breath before you have to before play  restarts  uh again maybe you're seeing the the  y-axis  it it isn't that big of a difference  we're talking about about 0.4 miles per  hour uh but it's non-zero and maybe it  wouldn't be the like if somewhere to  propose this rule wouldn't change  everything but it is something that we  would want to take into account  so  in terms of rule changes obviously that  the nfl is involved in that process so  this is something that we can use and  bring to the table and  i mean this is kind of the case with a  lot of things but when it comes to the  rule changes maybe shorting the play  clock it'll you'll have less dead time  you'll have more time when the players  are actually playing but then you're  going to have other effects such as  perhaps maybe the players aren't able to  reach their peak performance as much and  again we want  the game the players to be the games to  be as competitive as possible we want to  see the the athletes doing everything  they can  all right so  again here are the results um looking at  the that in this example um surface  clearly had had an effect i mean we saw  uh like some with an effect of about 0.8  miles per hour and then we saw that  baseball field um around negative point  eight so there definitely is an effect  um  in terms of max speed reached which is  pretty intuitive it makes sense you're  you're probably able to run a little bit  faster depending on what surface how  that what material you're running  through play clock there there was a  smaller effect but but a non-zero effect  um and  again we  from from this analysis we would come  back and say you know maybe shortening  the play clock would help in in some  case in some ways but it wouldn't be uh  it wouldn't be the end there would be a  negative effect to it possibly  and yeah so that is my presentation  thank you all for listening  [Applause]  thank you
 

